{-
   The three conversions - `lib/Core/Convert.yana`'s own file, heads and members verbatim.

   `::` is one operator over three classes, and which class relates a pair of types is the whole of
   the rule for what it does. `Widen` is the lossless direction and the only one that also happens
   without being written; `Narrow` is the lossy one, which must be; `Bitcast` is neither, being the
   same bits read as another type.

   Together in a file because the rule is the three of them: reading one of these without the other
   two tells you what a conversion does and not when it happens.

   **The instances are generated and the heads are not written here**, which is the one thing this
   file does not say and could not: the ladders are one instance per ordered pair of the widths a
   target has, so source would be generated source with the generator in the compiler anyway - the
   same fact in two places, with the copy in the library where a reader would trust it.
   `compiler/intrinsic/provided.def` is where the compiler claims them and
   `compiler/intrinsic/convert.h` is where which pair joins which class is decided, once, for both
   resolve stages.

   `@builtin(widen)`, `@builtin(narrow)` and `@builtin(bitcast)` are what bind these to the compiler,
   and `lib/Core/Convert.yana` writes the same three attributes on the same three declarations. The
   whole of the coupling is that the compiler can name each class and reach its one member by
   position - it holds no spelling of `widen`, `truncate` or `bitcast`.
-}

{-
   The lossless direction, and the only conversion that happens without being written.

   A `Widen` instance is required to be lossless and total; that is a contract on whoever writes one,
   checked no more than `Copy`'s is. Which of the two classes relates a pair of types is the whole of
   the rule for whether a conversion happens on its own or has to be written.

   **Two parameters and no functional dependency**, which is what makes the *result* type selecting:
   nothing about `widen(x)`'s argument says which rung was meant, so the position it stands in
   decides. `fn f(value: Int) -> Long = widen(value)` and `-> Double = widen(value)` are two
   different instances of one call.
-}
@builtin(widen)
pub class Widen(a, b):
  fn widen(from: a) -> b

{-
   The lossy direction, and the only way to spell it: `::` widens and selects, it does not remove
   data.

   The method is `truncate` rather than `narrow` because "narrow" says the type got smaller and
   "truncate" says what happened to the value, which is the part a reader needs. The class keeps its
   name so that the pair reads as one rule.

   The **identity** rung is part of this ladder rather than of `Widen`'s, and it is what makes
   `truncate` total over the types it relates: `Size` is `I64` on a sixty-four-bit machine and `Int`
   on a host whose word is thirty-two bits, so `truncate(length(xs)) :: Int` is a real truncation on
   one target and the identity on the other. Without a rung for the second case there is no portable
   spelling of "this length is an `Int` now". Nothing ever selects one implicitly or by `::`: both
   answer "the types are already the same" and return before an instance is looked for.
-}
@builtin(narrow)
pub class Narrow(a, b):
  fn truncate(from: a) -> b

{-
   The third thing a conversion can be: the same bits, read as another type.

   Two parameters and no functional dependency, which is `Widen`/`Narrow`'s shape exactly - so
   selection from context and `bitcast(x) :: Float` both work with no machinery of their own.

   **Instances only where the widths match on every target**, which is what makes this a class rather
   than a second `truncate`: every rung is generated over a same-width pair, so there are about
   twenty of them against the ladder's ninety and none of them can lose or invent a bit. A width the
   *target* answers pairs with the same target quantity and with nothing else - `Size` and `USize`
   are one word read two ways - and `WideInt` has no partner at all, being fifty-three bits in a
   sixty-four-bit register.
-}
@builtin(bitcast)
pub class Bitcast(a, b):
  fn bitcast(from: a) -> b
